---
description: How to write and change ArtScript (.art) code in this project
globs: **/*.art
alwaysApply: false
---

This project is written in ArtScript. Read `ARTSCRIPT.md` (the whole language, ~3.4K tokens) before writing code; `ARTSCRIPT-CORE.md` (~1.9K tokens, a whole app as the example) is enough for a small app or a canvas one. To only change existing code, `ARTSCRIPT-EDIT.md` (~800 tokens) is enough.

- After every change run `npx art check --ai` and fix what it reports (each error has `expected` and `fixes`).
- Read `npx art context <Component>` instead of whole files; prefer a small `art patch` to rewriting files.
- Write `test "..." { }` blocks for the main flows and run `npx art test`.
- Expressions are JavaScript; only the structure (`page`, `component`, `model`, `api`, `state`, `data`, `fn`, the view) is ArtScript's own.
- Write everything in `.art`: data, constants, helpers, formatting and calls to npm packages (`use "pkg" { x }`). Use a `.ts` file only for imperative code: code that drives the DOM or a canvas, or runs heavy loops (an animation or physics loop, canvas or WebGL drawing, a parser), and the wrapper of a library that owns its DOM (a map, a chart). Not in `.ts`: mock data, constants, date or money formatters, small wrappers around npm functions. What you import from `.ts` arrives as `Any`: the checker can't verify it, and `art check` warns (`MODULE_COULD_BE_ART`) when a module could be ArtScript.
